PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The panel maintains unanimous agreement that the clinical case represents valproate and lamotrigine co-administration leading to Stevens–Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis, with topiramate as the intermediate agent. The core distinction persists between viewing the initiating biochemical reaction as the upstream pharmacokinetic trigger—inhibition of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT)-mediated glucuronidation—versus the proximate cellular bioactivation step—cytochrome P450–mediated oxidation/epoxidation generating reactive arene oxides. Bridging both levels by designating glucuronidation inhibition as the pharmacokinetic initiator and CYP-mediated epoxidation as the proximate immunopathogenic reaction remains the most complete and robust conclusion.
}

